window.CHDATA=window.CHDATA||{};window.CHDATA[15]={"num": 15, "title": "Human Herpesviruses (HSV-1/2, VZV, EBV, CMV, HHV-6/7, HHV-8)", "keyPoints": "<ul>\n<li>The eight <strong>human herpesviruses</strong> are enveloped <strong>double-stranded DNA viruses</strong> that all establish <strong>lifelong latency</strong> after primary infection and can <strong>reactivate</strong>, especially when cell-mediated immunity falls<sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Alpha-herpesviruses</strong> (HSV-1, HSV-2, VZV) hide in <strong>sensory ganglia</strong> and cause <strong>mucocutaneous vesicular</strong> disease; <strong>beta</strong> (CMV, HHV-6, HHV-7) hide in <strong>myeloid/lymphoid cells</strong> and matter most in <strong>transplant recipients</strong> and the <strong>fetus</strong>; <strong>gamma</strong> (EBV, HHV-8) hide in <strong>B cells</strong> and are <strong>oncogenic</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>HSV encephalitis</strong> is the most important treatable viral encephalitis: start <strong>IV acyclovir as soon as it is suspected</strong>, and repeat a negative early CSF PCR before stopping<sup class=\"cite\">[4]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Zoster</strong> is VZV reactivation in a <strong>dermatome</strong>; start antivirals <strong>within 72 hours</strong>; <strong>Hutchinson sign</strong> means ophthalmology now. The <strong>recombinant zoster vaccine</strong> is non-live and can be given to immunocompromised adults<sup class=\"cite\">[5,6]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>EBV mononucleosis</strong>: fever, pharyngitis, posterior cervical nodes, splenomegaly, atypical lymphocytes. <strong>No antiviral</strong> therapy; <strong>no contact sports</strong> while the spleen is enlarged<sup class=\"cite\">[1,15]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>CMV</strong> is the key viral pathogen after <strong>transplantation</strong> (highest risk <strong>D+/R−</strong> in solid organ, <strong>R+</strong> in stem cell transplant) and the <strong>commonest congenital infection</strong> and leading non-genetic cause of <strong>sensorineural hearing loss</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[8,9,12]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>HHV-8</strong> causes <strong>Kaposi sarcoma</strong>, primary effusion lymphoma and multicentric Castleman disease; <strong>HHV-6B</strong> causes <strong>exanthema subitum</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[16,17]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>", "redFlags": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Suspected HSV encephalitis = IV acyclovir now</strong>, not after the MRI or the PCR. A <strong>negative CSF PCR in the first 72 hours</strong> does not exclude it: <strong>repeat</strong> it.</li>\n<li><strong>Tzanck smear</strong> cannot tell HSV from VZV: use <strong>PCR</strong> of the lesion.</li>\n<li><strong>HSV IgM is useless</strong>; <strong>low-positive HSV-2 IgG needs confirmation</strong>; do <strong>not screen</strong> the general population.</li>\n<li><strong>Neonatal HSV risk is highest with primary maternal infection near term</strong>, not with recurrences.</li>\n<li><strong>Hutchinson sign</strong> (nose tip) = possible eye involvement = <strong>urgent ophthalmology</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Zoster antivirals within 72 hours</strong>; <strong>brivudine + 5-FU/capecitabine can kill</strong> (DPD inhibition).</li>\n<li><strong>Varicella in adults and pregnancy</strong> can cause severe <strong>pneumonia</strong>; maternal rash <strong>5 days before to 2 days after delivery</strong> = neonate at high risk → <strong>VZIG</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Monospot negative?</strong> Early illness or a child under 4: use EBV serology; if still negative, think <strong>CMV, acute HIV, toxoplasmosis</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Amoxicillin rash in mononucleosis</strong> is not a true penicillin allergy; <strong>no contact sports</strong> while the spleen is enlarged.</li>\n<li><strong>CMV highest risk: D+/R− in SOT, R+ in HSCT.</strong> <strong>GI CMV disease</strong> can occur with <strong>negative blood PCR</strong>: biopsy.</li>\n<li><strong>Ganciclovir → neutropenia; foscarnet/cidofovir → kidneys.</strong> <strong>Letermovir has no HSV/VZV activity</strong> and is for <strong>prophylaxis</strong> only.</li>\n<li><strong>Congenital CMV</strong>: test <strong>urine/saliva within 21 days</strong>; asymptomatic babies can still lose hearing later.</li>\n<li><strong>Classic Kaposi sarcoma</strong> occurs in <strong>older Mediterranean men</strong>, including in Italy: not only in AIDS.</li>\n</ul>", "intro": "", "sections": [{"id": "1", "title": "Microbiology", "html": "", "subs": [{"id": "1.1", "title": "Shared features of the family <em>Orthoherpesviridae</em>", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Large linear double-stranded DNA</strong> genome inside an <strong>icosahedral capsid</strong>, surrounded by a protein <strong>tegument</strong> and a lipid <strong>envelope</strong> with glycoproteins<sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup>.</li>\n<li>The envelope makes them <strong>fragile in the environment</strong>: transmission needs <strong>close contact</strong> with secretions, lesions, blood, organs or (for VZV) <strong>airborne</strong> particles.</li>\n<li><strong>Latency</strong> is the defining property: the viral genome persists as an episome with very limited gene expression; <strong>reactivation</strong> produces recurrent disease or asymptomatic shedding<sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Antiviral targets:</strong> all approved drugs except letermovir and maribavir act on the <strong>viral DNA polymerase</strong> (section 6.1).</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "1.2", "title": "Classification", "html": "<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Subfamily</th>\n<th>Virus (official name)</th>\n<th>Site of latency</th>\n<th>Key biological feature</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Alpha</strong></td>\n<td><strong>HSV-1</strong> (HHV-1), <strong>HSV-2</strong> (HHV-2)</td>\n<td><strong>Sensory ganglia</strong> (trigeminal for HSV-1, sacral for HSV-2 typically)</td>\n<td>Short replication cycle, <strong>cytolytic</strong>; vesicles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td><strong>VZV</strong> (HHV-3)</td>\n<td><strong>Dorsal root and cranial nerve ganglia</strong></td>\n<td>Airborne primary infection (varicella); dermatomal reactivation (zoster)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Beta</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CMV</strong> (HHV-5)</td>\n<td><strong>Monocytes and myeloid progenitors</strong></td>\n<td>Slow cycle; <strong>cytomegaly</strong> with <strong>\"owl's eye\" intranuclear inclusions</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td><strong>HHV-6A, HHV-6B</strong>, <strong>HHV-7</strong></td>\n<td>T lymphocytes, monocytes; HHV-6 can <strong>integrate into chromosomes</strong></td>\n<td>Roseoloviruses</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Gamma</strong></td>\n<td><strong>EBV</strong> (HHV-4)</td>\n<td><strong>Memory B cells</strong></td>\n<td>Drives B-cell proliferation; <strong>oncogenic</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td><strong>HHV-8</strong> (Kaposi sarcoma-associated herpesvirus, KSHV)</td>\n<td>B cells, endothelial cells</td>\n<td><strong>Oncogenic</strong> (Kaposi sarcoma, lymphomas)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>"}]}, {"id": "2", "title": "Epidemiology", "html": "", "subs": [{"id": "2.1", "title": "Overview by virus", "html": "<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Virus</th>\n<th>Transmission</th>\n<th>Seroprevalence and burden</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>HSV-1</strong></td>\n<td>Saliva and oral lesions, often in childhood; <strong>oral-genital sex</strong></td>\n<td>About <strong>two-thirds of people under 50</strong> worldwide<sup class=\"cite\">[18]</sup>. A growing share of <strong>first-episode genital herpes</strong> in young adults is now due to HSV-1<sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>HSV-2</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Sexual</strong>, mostly during <strong>asymptomatic shedding</strong>; <strong>vertical</strong> (neonatal herpes)</td>\n<td>About <strong>13% of people aged 15 to 49</strong> worldwide<sup class=\"cite\">[18]</sup>; HSV-2 roughly <strong>triples the risk of HIV acquisition</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[18]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>VZV</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Airborne</strong> and contact with vesicle fluid; zoster lesions transmit varicella (not zoster) to non-immune contacts</td>\n<td>Varicella: near-universal childhood infection where there is no vaccination. Zoster: lifetime risk about <strong>1 in 3</strong>, rising steeply with <strong>age</strong> and <strong>immunosuppression</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[5]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>EBV</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Saliva</strong> (\"kissing disease\"); rarely transplant/blood</td>\n<td>About <strong>90% of adults</strong> infected; incubation of symptomatic infection about <strong>6 weeks</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[15]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>CMV</strong></td>\n<td>Saliva, <strong>urine of young children</strong>, sexual, <strong>breast milk</strong>, <strong>transplacental</strong>, <strong>organ/stem-cell transplant</strong>, blood</td>\n<td>Seroprevalence varies widely with age, region and socioeconomic level. Congenital infection affects about <strong>0.64% of live births</strong> worldwide<sup class=\"cite\">[12]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>HHV-6B / HHV-7</strong></td>\n<td>Saliva, in <strong>infancy</strong></td>\n<td>Near-universal by early childhood; about <strong>1%</strong> of people carry <strong>chromosomally integrated HHV-6 (ciHHV-6)</strong> inherited through the germline<sup class=\"cite\">[16]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>HHV-8</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Saliva</strong>; sexual (notably among <strong>men who have sex with men</strong>); transplant</td>\n<td>High in <strong>sub-Saharan Africa</strong>, intermediate in the <strong>Mediterranean</strong> (including Italy), low in northern Europe and the USA<sup class=\"cite\">[17]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>"}, {"id": "2.2", "title": "Groups at risk of severe disease", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Neonates</strong>: neonatal HSV, neonatal varicella, congenital CMV and varicella<sup class=\"cite\">[1,12]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: varicella pneumonia; fetal infection by CMV and VZV.</li>\n<li><strong>Impaired cell-mediated immunity</strong>: <strong>HIV</strong> with low CD4 counts, <strong>solid organ (SOT)</strong> and <strong>hematopoietic stem cell transplant (HSCT)</strong> recipients, hematological malignancy, high-dose corticosteroids and other immunosuppressants<sup class=\"cite\">[8,9]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>CMV serostatus defines transplant risk</strong>: in <strong>SOT</strong> the highest risk is the <strong>seronegative recipient of a seropositive organ (D+/R−)</strong>, who meets the virus for the first time; in <strong>allogeneic HSCT</strong> the highest risk is the <strong>seropositive recipient (R+)</strong>, whose latent virus reactivates while donor-derived immunity is rebuilt<sup class=\"cite\">[8,9]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}]}, {"id": "3", "title": "Pathogenesis", "html": "", "subs": [{"id": "3.1", "title": "The common cycle: primary infection, latency, reactivation", "html": "<ol>\n<li><strong>Primary infection</strong> at a mucosal or respiratory surface, with local replication.</li>\n<li><strong>Spread to the latency site</strong>: alpha-herpesviruses travel <strong>retrogradely along sensory axons</strong> to the ganglia; beta and gamma viruses spread in <strong>leukocytes</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Latency</strong> for life, kept in check by <strong>T-cell immunity</strong> (CD8+ cytotoxic T cells above all).</li>\n<li><strong>Reactivation</strong> with stress, fever, UV light, age-related immune decline or immunosuppression. Alpha viruses travel back <strong>anterogradely</strong> to the skin of the same dermatome; CMV reactivates in tissues and blood.</li>\n</ol>"}, {"id": "3.2", "title": "Virus-specific mechanisms", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>HSV</strong>: replicates in epithelial cells, causing <strong>ballooning degeneration</strong>, <strong>multinucleated giant cells</strong> and <strong>intraepithelial vesicles</strong>. <strong>HSV encephalitis</strong> in adults is mostly HSV-1 and affects the <strong>temporal and orbitofrontal lobes</strong> with hemorrhagic necrosis. <strong>Neonatal HSV</strong> is acquired mostly <strong>during delivery</strong>; the risk is highest when the mother has a <strong>primary infection near term</strong> (no protective maternal antibodies yet) and low with recurrent infection<sup class=\"cite\">[2,3]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>VZV</strong>: inhaled virus replicates in the upper respiratory tract and regional lymph nodes, then <strong>two viremic phases</strong> carry it to the skin after an incubation of about <strong>10 to 21 days</strong>. In zoster, reactivation inflames the <strong>ganglion and nerve</strong> (hence pain before the rash, and <strong>postherpetic neuralgia</strong>)<sup class=\"cite\">[5]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>EBV</strong>: infects <strong>B cells</strong> via <strong>CD21</strong>. The clinical picture of mononucleosis is due mostly to the <strong>massive CD8+ T-cell response</strong> against infected B cells (the \"atypical lymphocytes\"). Latency proteins (EBNA, LMP-1) can drive <strong>lymphoproliferation</strong> when T-cell control fails, as in <strong>post-transplant lymphoproliferative disorder (PTLD)</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[1,15]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>CMV</strong>: causes <strong>cytomegaly</strong> and tissue injury (lung, gut, liver, retina) and also <strong>indirect effects</strong> in transplant recipients (immunomodulation, more bacterial and fungal infections, graft rejection)<sup class=\"cite\">[8]</sup>. Fetal infection follows <strong>maternal primary infection</strong> (highest fetal risk of serious sequelae when it occurs <strong>around conception or in the first trimester</strong>) or non-primary infection<sup class=\"cite\">[12]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>HHV-8</strong>: viral genes (for example LANA, viral cyclin, viral FLIP, viral IL-6) promote <strong>cell survival, proliferation and angiogenesis</strong> of infected endothelial cells, producing the spindle-cell vascular lesions of Kaposi sarcoma<sup class=\"cite\">[17]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}]}, {"id": "4", "title": "Signs and Symptoms", "html": "", "subs": [{"id": "4.1", "title": "HSV-1 and HSV-2", "html": "<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Syndrome</th>\n<th>Main features</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Herpes labialis</strong> (cold sores)</td>\n<td>Prodrome of tingling, then <strong>grouped vesicles on an erythematous base</strong> at the vermilion border, crusting in about a week</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Primary gingivostomatitis</strong></td>\n<td>Children: fever, painful oral ulcers, drooling, cervical nodes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Genital herpes</strong></td>\n<td><strong>First episode</strong>: painful multiple <strong>vesicles and ulcers</strong>, inguinal nodes, fever, dysuria, sometimes <strong>aseptic meningitis</strong> or sacral radiculopathy (urinary retention). <strong>Recurrences</strong> are milder and shorter; HSV-2 recurs far more often than genital HSV-1<sup class=\"cite\">[2,3]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Herpetic whitlow</strong>, <strong>herpes gladiatorum</strong></td>\n<td>Finger or skin infection by direct inoculation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Eczema herpeticum</strong> (Kaposi varicelliform eruption)</td>\n<td>Widespread HSV on <strong>atopic dermatitis</strong>; can be severe</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>HSV keratitis</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Dendritic corneal ulcer</strong>; a leading infectious cause of corneal blindness</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>HSV encephalitis</strong></td>\n<td>Fever, headache, <strong>altered behavior or consciousness</strong>, <strong>focal deficits</strong>, <strong>seizures</strong>, aphasia, olfactory hallucinations (temporal lobe). Untreated mortality is about <strong>70%</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Neonatal HSV</strong></td>\n<td>Three forms: <strong>skin-eye-mouth</strong>, <strong>CNS</strong>, <strong>disseminated</strong> (hepatitis, pneumonitis, DIC). May appear <strong>without skin lesions</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Immunocompromised</strong></td>\n<td>Chronic, enlarging ulcers; esophagitis; dissemination</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>HSV is also a common trigger of <strong>erythema multiforme</strong>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "VZV", "html": "<p><strong>Varicella (chickenpox)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mild prodrome, then a <strong>pruritic rash</strong> that starts on the <strong>trunk and face</strong> (centripetal), evolving from macules to papules to <strong>vesicles (\"dew drop on a rose petal\")</strong> to crusts. Lesions in <strong>all stages at the same time</strong> in one area are typical.</li>\n<li>Contagious from <strong>1 to 2 days before the rash</strong> until <strong>all lesions have crusted</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[19]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Complications</strong>: <strong>bacterial superinfection</strong> (<em>S. aureus</em>, <strong>group A streptococcus</strong>, including necrotizing fasciitis); <strong>varicella pneumonia</strong> (adults, <strong>pregnant women</strong>, smokers, immunocompromised); <strong>acute cerebellar ataxia</strong>; encephalitis; <strong>Reye syndrome</strong> with aspirin; hepatitis<sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Congenital varicella syndrome</strong> (limb hypoplasia, cicatricial skin scars, eye and brain defects) after maternal infection in the <strong>first 20 weeks</strong>, with a low absolute risk<sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Neonatal varicella</strong> is most severe when the mother's rash starts <strong>5 days before to 2 days after delivery</strong>: the baby gets the virus but not yet maternal antibodies<sup class=\"cite\">[20]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Herpes zoster (shingles)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Prodromal pain</strong>, burning or itching in a dermatome, then <strong>unilateral grouped vesicles</strong> that <strong>do not cross the midline</strong>; <strong>thoracic</strong> dermatomes are the most common<sup class=\"cite\">[5]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Herpes zoster ophthalmicus</strong> (V1): <strong>Hutchinson sign</strong>, vesicles on the <strong>tip of the nose</strong> (nasociliary nerve), predicts <strong>eye involvement</strong> (keratitis, uveitis)<sup class=\"cite\">[5]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Ramsay Hunt syndrome</strong>: <strong>geniculate ganglion</strong>; <strong>facial palsy</strong> with vesicles in the <strong>ear canal/pinna</strong>, hearing loss, vertigo.</li>\n<li><strong>Postherpetic neuralgia (PHN)</strong>: pain persisting <strong>≥90 days</strong> after the rash; the <strong>most common complication</strong>, more frequent with <strong>older age</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[5]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Immunocompromised</strong>: <strong>disseminated zoster</strong> (lesions beyond adjacent dermatomes, visceral involvement: pneumonitis, hepatitis, encephalitis). Other complications: <strong>VZV vasculopathy</strong> (stroke), myelitis, <strong>zoster sine herpete</strong> (pain without rash).</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "4.3", "title": "EBV", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Infectious mononucleosis</strong> (adolescents and young adults): <strong>fever</strong>, <strong>exudative pharyngitis</strong>, <strong>lymphadenopathy</strong> (typically <strong>posterior cervical</strong>), <strong>fatigue</strong>, <strong>splenomegaly</strong>, sometimes hepatomegaly, palatal petechiae and eyelid edema<sup class=\"cite\">[15]</sup>.</li>\n<li>Primary infection in <strong>young children</strong> is usually <strong>asymptomatic</strong> or non-specific<sup class=\"cite\">[15]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Ampicillin/amoxicillin rash</strong>: a maculopapular rash in most patients with mononucleosis who receive these drugs; it does not usually mean a true penicillin allergy<sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Complications</strong>: <strong>splenic rupture</strong> (rare, usually in the first weeks), <strong>upper airway obstruction</strong> from tonsillar swelling, <strong>autoimmune hemolytic anemia</strong> and thrombocytopenia, hepatitis, neurological syndromes (Guillain-Barré, encephalitis), <strong>hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis (HLH)</strong>, and <strong>chronic active EBV</strong> disease<sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>EBV-associated tumors</strong>: <strong>endemic Burkitt lymphoma</strong>, <strong>nasopharyngeal carcinoma</strong>, a share of <strong>Hodgkin lymphoma</strong>, extranodal <strong>NK/T-cell lymphoma</strong>, EBV-positive gastric carcinoma, <strong>PTLD</strong>, and HIV-associated lymphomas; <strong>oral hairy leukoplakia</strong> in HIV<sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "4.4", "title": "CMV", "html": "<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Setting</th>\n<th>Clinical picture</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Immunocompetent host</strong></td>\n<td>Mostly asymptomatic; or a <strong>mononucleosis-like syndrome</strong> with <strong>negative heterophile test</strong>, fever, mild hepatitis, atypical lymphocytes, often without pharyngitis<sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Transplant recipients</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CMV infection</strong> (DNAemia only) vs <strong>CMV disease</strong>: <strong>CMV syndrome</strong> (fever, malaise, leukopenia, thrombocytopenia) or <strong>tissue-invasive disease</strong>: <strong>pneumonitis</strong> (most severe after HSCT), <strong>gastrointestinal disease</strong> (colitis, esophagitis, ulcers), hepatitis, retinitis, and indirect effects on the graft<sup class=\"cite\">[8,9]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Advanced HIV</strong> (CD4 usually &lt;50 cells/µL)</td>\n<td><strong>CMV retinitis</strong> (floaters, visual loss; \"pizza pie\" fundus with hemorrhages and exudates), colitis, esophagitis, polyradiculopathy<sup class=\"cite\">[21]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Congenital CMV</strong></td>\n<td>About <strong>10 to 15%</strong> symptomatic at birth: <strong>microcephaly</strong>, intracranial (periventricular) <strong>calcifications</strong>, <strong>petechiae</strong> (\"blueberry muffin\"), hepatosplenomegaly, jaundice, chorioretinitis, <strong>sensorineural hearing loss (SNHL)</strong>. Asymptomatic infants can still develop <strong>late or progressive SNHL</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[12]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>"}, {"id": "4.5", "title": "HHV-6, HHV-7 and HHV-8", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>HHV-6B</strong>: <strong>exanthema subitum</strong> (roseola infantum, \"sixth disease\"): <strong>high fever for 3 to 5 days</strong>, then a pink maculopapular rash <strong>as the fever falls</strong>; a common cause of <strong>febrile seizures</strong> in infants. In <strong>HSCT</strong> recipients (especially after <strong>cord blood</strong> transplant), reactivation can cause <strong>limbic encephalitis</strong> (memory loss, confusion, seizures, hyponatremia)<sup class=\"cite\">[16]</sup>. HHV-6 reactivation is also linked to <strong>DRESS</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[16]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>HHV-7</strong>: a less common cause of roseola; associated with <strong>pityriasis rosea</strong> (causal role debated)<sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>HHV-8</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[17]</sup>:</li>\n<li><strong>Kaposi sarcoma (KS)</strong>: violaceous <strong>macules, plaques or nodules</strong> on skin and mucosa (palate), sometimes lymph nodes, lung and gut. Four epidemiological forms: <strong>classic</strong> (older men of <strong>Mediterranean</strong> or Eastern European origin, indolent, lower limbs), <strong>endemic</strong> (sub-Saharan Africa), <strong>iatrogenic</strong> (transplant recipients), <strong>epidemic/AIDS-associated</strong> (AIDS-defining).</li>\n<li><strong>Primary effusion lymphoma</strong> (lymphomatous pleural, pericardial or peritoneal effusions), <strong>multicentric Castleman disease</strong>, and the <strong>KSHV inflammatory cytokine syndrome (KICS)</strong>.</li>\n</ul>"}]}, {"id": "5", "title": "Diagnosis", "html": "", "subs": [{"id": "5.1", "title": "When to suspect", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Grouped vesicles or painful ulcers</strong> on skin or mucosa (HSV, VZV).</li>\n<li><strong>Fever with altered mental status or focal signs</strong> (HSV encephalitis: treat first, then test)<sup class=\"cite\">[4]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Fever, pharyngitis, lymphadenopathy and atypical lymphocytes</strong> in a young adult (EBV; if the heterophile test is negative, think of CMV, acute HIV, toxoplasmosis).</li>\n<li><strong>Fever, cytopenias, raised transaminases, diarrhea, pneumonitis or graft dysfunction</strong> in a <strong>transplant recipient</strong> (CMV)<sup class=\"cite\">[8,9]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Failed newborn hearing screen</strong>, microcephaly or growth restriction in an infant (congenital CMV)<sup class=\"cite\">[12]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Violaceous skin or palatal lesions</strong> in a person with HIV or a transplant (KS).</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "5.2", "title": "Laboratory tools by virus", "html": "<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Virus / syndrome</th>\n<th>Test of choice</th>\n<th>Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Mucocutaneous HSV/VZV</strong></td>\n<td><strong>PCR (NAAT) of a swab from the base of an unroofed vesicle or ulcer</strong></td>\n<td>Most sensitive; distinguishes HSV-1, HSV-2 and VZV. <strong>Viral culture</strong> is less sensitive; the <strong>Tzanck smear</strong> (multinucleated giant cells) is insensitive and <strong>does not distinguish HSV from VZV</strong>: not recommended<sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Genital herpes serology</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Type-specific IgG</strong> (glycoprotein G based)</td>\n<td>For selected cases (recurrent or atypical lesions with negative PCR, partner of a person with genital herpes). <strong>IgM is not useful</strong>. Low-positive HSV-2 index values (1.1 to 3.0 on a common EIA) are often false positive: <strong>confirm with a second test</strong> (two-step testing). <strong>Screening the general population is not recommended</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>HSV encephalitis</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CSF HSV PCR</strong></td>\n<td>Highly sensitive and specific, but can be <strong>negative in the first 72 hours</strong>: if suspicion persists, <strong>repeat the lumbar puncture within 3 to 7 days</strong> and continue acyclovir<sup class=\"cite\">[1,4]</sup>. CSF: lymphocytic pleocytosis, raised protein, often red cells. <strong>MRI</strong>: temporal lobe hyperintensity (T2/FLAIR, diffusion); <strong>EEG</strong>: temporal periodic discharges. Do <strong>not replace</strong> pathogen-specific CSF PCR with multiplex panels<sup class=\"cite\">[4]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Neonatal HSV</strong></td>\n<td>PCR of surface swabs, blood and CSF; ALT</td>\n<td>Blood PCR is useful in all forms</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Varicella / zoster</strong></td>\n<td>Usually <strong>clinical</strong>; <strong>PCR</strong> of lesion swabs for atypical cases; CSF PCR for CNS disease</td>\n<td>VZV IgG to check <strong>immune status</strong> (exposures, pregnancy, before transplant)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>EBV mononucleosis</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Heterophile antibody test</strong> (Monospot) + blood count (lymphocytosis with <strong>&gt;10% atypical lymphocytes</strong>)</td>\n<td>Heterophile test is often <strong>negative early</strong> and in <strong>children under 4 years</strong>: then use <strong>EBV-specific serology</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[15]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>EBV in transplant</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Quantitative EBV DNA in blood</strong></td>\n<td>Monitoring for PTLD in high-risk recipients (EBV-seronegative recipient); diagnosis of PTLD needs <strong>biopsy</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>CMV in transplant</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Quantitative PCR (QNAT)</strong> in plasma or whole blood, reported in <strong>IU/mL</strong> (WHO international standard)</td>\n<td>Same assay and sample type over time, since values are not interchangeable. <strong>Tissue-invasive disease</strong> (especially <strong>GI</strong>) is confirmed by <strong>histology/immunohistochemistry</strong>, and blood PCR can be <strong>negative</strong>. <strong>CMV-specific T-cell immunity</strong> assays can help stratify risk<sup class=\"cite\">[8,9]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>CMV retinitis</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Fundoscopy</strong> by an ophthalmologist</td>\n<td>Clinical diagnosis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Maternal CMV</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CMV IgG and IgM + IgG avidity</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Low avidity</strong> = primary infection within the last ~3 months. IgM alone is unreliable (persists, false positives)<sup class=\"cite\">[12]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Fetal CMV</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Amniotic fluid PCR</strong></td>\n<td>Performed <strong>≥20 weeks</strong> of gestation and <strong>≥8 weeks</strong> after maternal infection<sup class=\"cite\">[12]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Congenital CMV (newborn)</strong></td>\n<td><strong>CMV PCR on urine or saliva within the first 21 days</strong></td>\n<td>After 3 weeks, positivity may reflect <strong>postnatal</strong> infection (breast milk). Confirm a positive saliva result with urine; dried blood spot PCR allows retrospective diagnosis<sup class=\"cite\">[12]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>HHV-6</strong></td>\n<td>Quantitative PCR</td>\n<td>Very high, persistent loads in whole blood or in hair follicles suggest <strong>ciHHV-6</strong> rather than active infection<sup class=\"cite\">[16]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Kaposi sarcoma</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Biopsy</strong> with immunohistochemistry for <strong>HHV-8 LANA</strong></td>\n<td>Staging, HIV test<sup class=\"cite\">[17]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>"}, {"id": "5.3", "title": "EBV serology: how to read it", "html": "<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Pattern</th>\n<th>VCA IgM</th>\n<th>VCA IgG</th>\n<th>EBNA IgG</th>\n<th>Interpretation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Seronegative</td>\n<td>−</td>\n<td>−</td>\n<td>−</td>\n<td>Never infected (susceptible)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Acute primary infection</strong></td>\n<td><strong>+</strong></td>\n<td><strong>+</strong></td>\n<td><strong>−</strong></td>\n<td>Mononucleosis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Past infection</td>\n<td>−</td>\n<td>+</td>\n<td><strong>+</strong></td>\n<td><strong>EBNA IgG</strong> appears weeks to months after primary infection; its presence argues <strong>against</strong> acute primary infection</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>"}]}, {"id": "6", "title": "Therapy", "html": "", "subs": [{"id": "6.1", "title": "Antiviral drugs and how they work", "html": "<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Drug</th>\n<th>Mechanism</th>\n<th>Main spectrum and uses</th>\n<th>Key toxicity / pitfalls</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Acyclovir</strong> (IV/oral), <strong>valacyclovir</strong> (oral prodrug), <strong>famciclovir</strong> (oral prodrug of penciclovir)</td>\n<td>Phosphorylated first by <strong>viral thymidine kinase (TK)</strong>, then by cellular kinases; inhibits viral DNA polymerase</td>\n<td><strong>HSV, VZV</strong>; acyclovir IV for encephalitis, neonatal HSV, severe or disseminated disease</td>\n<td><strong>Crystal nephropathy</strong> (hydrate, adjust for renal function); neurotoxicity in renal failure. Resistance mainly via <strong>TK mutations</strong> in immunocompromised hosts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Brivudine</strong> (oral, available in Italy and some European countries)</td>\n<td>Nucleoside analogue</td>\n<td><strong>Zoster</strong> in immunocompetent adults</td>\n<td><strong>Contraindicated with fluoropyrimidines</strong> (5-fluorouracil, capecitabine, also topical 5-FU, flucytosine): brivudine inhibits <strong>DPD</strong>, causing <strong>fatal fluoropyrimidine toxicity</strong>. Wait <strong>at least 4 weeks</strong> after brivudine before starting them<sup class=\"cite\">[22]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Ganciclovir</strong> (IV), <strong>valganciclovir</strong> (oral prodrug)</td>\n<td>Phosphorylated first by the <strong>CMV UL97 kinase</strong>; inhibits DNA polymerase (UL54)</td>\n<td><strong>CMV</strong> treatment, prophylaxis and pre-emptive therapy; HHV-6</td>\n<td><strong>Myelosuppression</strong> (neutropenia), teratogenic. Resistance via <strong>UL97</strong> (most common) or <strong>UL54</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Foscarnet</strong> (IV)</td>\n<td>Directly blocks the polymerase pyrophosphate site; <strong>no activation needed</strong></td>\n<td>Ganciclovir-resistant CMV; <strong>acyclovir-resistant HSV/VZV</strong>; HHV-6 encephalitis</td>\n<td><strong>Nephrotoxicity</strong>, <strong>electrolyte disturbances</strong> (hypocalcemia, hypomagnesemia, hypokalemia, hypophosphatemia) with seizures, genital ulcers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Cidofovir</strong> (IV)</td>\n<td>Nucleotide analogue, needs only cellular kinases</td>\n<td>Resistant CMV or HSV</td>\n<td><strong>Severe nephrotoxicity</strong> (given with probenecid and hydration), neutropenia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Letermovir</strong></td>\n<td>Inhibits the <strong>CMV terminase complex</strong> (UL56)</td>\n<td><strong>CMV prophylaxis only</strong> (HSCT; kidney transplant)</td>\n<td><strong>No activity against HSV/VZV</strong>, so acyclovir prophylaxis must be added; not myelotoxic<sup class=\"cite\">[8,10]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Maribavir</strong></td>\n<td>Inhibits the <strong>UL97 kinase</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Refractory/resistant CMV</strong> after transplant</td>\n<td>Taste disturbance; <strong>antagonizes ganciclovir</strong>; not for CNS disease or retinitis<sup class=\"cite\">[11]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>"}, {"id": "6.2", "title": "HSV", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Genital herpes, first episode</strong>: oral acyclovir, valacyclovir or famciclovir <strong>for all patients</strong>, because first episodes can be severe and prolonged<sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Recurrent genital herpes</strong>: either <strong>episodic therapy</strong> (started <strong>within 1 day of lesion onset</strong> or during the prodrome) or <strong>daily suppressive therapy</strong>, which reduces recurrences by about <strong>70 to 80%</strong> and lowers <strong>transmission</strong> to partners<sup class=\"cite\">[2,23]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Herpes labialis</strong>: often no treatment; short oral courses shorten episodes if started early.</li>\n<li><strong>HSV encephalitis</strong>: <strong>IV acyclovir started immediately</strong> on suspicion, <strong>before</strong> test results, for <strong>14 to 21 days</strong> in confirmed cases<sup class=\"cite\">[1,4]</sup>. The EAN/ESCMID 2026 guideline could not recommend for or against <strong>corticosteroids</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[4]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Neonatal HSV</strong>: <strong>IV acyclovir</strong>, followed by oral suppression after CNS disease<sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>HSV keratitis</strong>: topical/oral antivirals with ophthalmology; <strong>topical corticosteroids are contraindicated in epithelial (dendritic) keratitis</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Acyclovir-resistant HSV</strong> (immunocompromised): <strong>foscarnet</strong> or cidofovir<sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "6.3", "title": "VZV", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Varicella</strong>: oral antivirals for patients at risk of severe disease (adolescents, adults, chronic skin or lung disease, long-term salicylates or steroids, pregnancy), ideally <strong>within 24 hours</strong> of rash onset; <strong>IV acyclovir</strong> for immunocompromised patients, pneumonia, encephalitis. <strong>Avoid aspirin</strong> (Reye syndrome)<sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Zoster</strong> (immunocompetent): <strong>oral valacyclovir, famciclovir or acyclovir</strong> (or brivudine), started <strong>within 72 hours</strong> of rash onset; treat later if new lesions are still appearing or the eye is involved. Goals: faster healing, less acute pain<sup class=\"cite\">[5]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>IV acyclovir</strong> for <strong>disseminated zoster</strong>, <strong>severe immunosuppression</strong>, <strong>CNS</strong> involvement and complicated <strong>ophthalmic</strong> disease; <strong>urgent ophthalmology</strong> for herpes zoster ophthalmicus<sup class=\"cite\">[5]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Pain</strong>: analgesics for acute pain. For <strong>PHN</strong>: <strong>gabapentinoids</strong>, <strong>tricyclic antidepressants</strong>, <strong>topical lidocaine</strong> or <strong>capsaicin</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[5]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "6.4", "title": "EBV", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Supportive care</strong> (rest, antipyretics, analgesics, hydration). <strong>Antivirals are not recommended</strong> for uncomplicated mononucleosis<sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Corticosteroids</strong> only for complications: <strong>impending airway obstruction</strong>, severe hemolytic anemia or thrombocytopenia<sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>PTLD</strong>: <strong>reduce immunosuppression</strong>, anti-CD20 <strong>rituximab</strong>, chemotherapy if needed<sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "6.5", "title": "CMV", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Treatment of CMV disease</strong> in transplant recipients: <strong>IV ganciclovir</strong> (severe disease, high viral load, GI absorption doubtful) or <strong>oral valganciclovir</strong> (mild to moderate disease); <strong>reduce immunosuppression</strong> where possible. Continue until <strong>symptoms resolve</strong> and <strong>viral load falls below threshold</strong> on one or two consecutive weekly samples, with a minimum of about <strong>2 weeks</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[8]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Refractory CMV</strong> (viral load rising or persisting after <strong>≥2 weeks</strong> of appropriate therapy): send <strong>genotypic resistance testing</strong> (UL97, UL54) and switch to <strong>foscarnet</strong>, or <strong>maribavir</strong> for refractory infection with or without resistance<sup class=\"cite\">[8,11]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>CMV retinitis in HIV</strong>: anti-CMV therapy plus <strong>antiretroviral therapy</strong>, with ophthalmology follow-up<sup class=\"cite\">[21]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Congenital CMV</strong>: <strong>oral valganciclovir for 6 months</strong>, started within the first month of life, in neonates with <strong>moderate to severe symptomatic</strong> disease; 6 months gave modestly better hearing and developmental outcomes than 6 weeks<sup class=\"cite\">[12,14]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "6.6", "title": "HHV-6 and HHV-8", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>HHV-6 encephalitis</strong> after HSCT: <strong>foscarnet or ganciclovir</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[16]</sup>. Exanthema subitum needs only supportive care.</li>\n<li><strong>Kaposi sarcoma</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[17]</sup>:</li>\n<li><strong>AIDS-related</strong>: <strong>antiretroviral therapy</strong> first, which often makes early KS regress; <strong>chemotherapy</strong> (pegylated liposomal doxorubicin, paclitaxel) for advanced disease.</li>\n<li><strong>Transplant-related</strong>: <strong>reduce immunosuppression</strong>; switching to an <strong>mTOR inhibitor</strong> (sirolimus) can help.</li>\n<li><strong>Classic KS</strong>: local treatments (radiotherapy, excision, intralesional therapy) or systemic chemotherapy for extensive disease.</li>\n</ul>"}]}, {"id": "7", "title": "Follow-up", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Genital herpes</strong>: counsel on the <strong>natural history</strong> (recurrences, asymptomatic shedding), <strong>condoms</strong>, informing partners, and the option of <strong>suppressive therapy</strong>; offer <strong>HIV and STI testing</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup>. Reassess the need for suppression periodically.</li>\n<li><strong>Herpes in pregnancy</strong>: women with a history of genital herpes receive <strong>suppressive therapy from 36 weeks</strong>; <strong>caesarean delivery</strong> if there are <strong>genital lesions or prodromal symptoms at labor</strong>. The newborn exposed to a primary maternal infection needs evaluation by the neonatologist<sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>HSV encephalitis</strong>: neurocognitive deficits are common (memory, behavior) and need <strong>rehabilitation</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[4]</sup>. A <strong>neurological relapse weeks after HSV encephalitis</strong> may be <strong>autoimmune (anti-NMDA receptor) encephalitis</strong>, not viral relapse<sup class=\"cite\">[4]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Zoster</strong>: follow pain to identify <strong>PHN</strong>; ophthalmology follow-up in HZ ophthalmicus. Zoster in a young person without obvious cause should prompt an <strong>HIV test</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Mononucleosis</strong>: fatigue can last weeks. <strong>Avoid contact sports and heavy lifting</strong> for at least <strong>3 to 4 weeks</strong> from symptom onset, and longer while the spleen is enlarged<sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>CMV after transplant</strong>: during <strong>pre-emptive therapy</strong>, <strong>weekly CMV QNAT</strong>; after treatment, watch for <strong>recurrence</strong>, especially in D+/R− recipients and after rejection treatment. <strong>Rising viral load after ≥2 weeks</strong> of therapy → resistance testing<sup class=\"cite\">[8,9]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Congenital CMV</strong>: <strong>audiological follow-up through early childhood</strong> (hearing loss may be <strong>late-onset or progressive</strong>), neurodevelopmental and ophthalmological follow-up; <strong>blood counts</strong> during valganciclovir (neutropenia)<sup class=\"cite\">[12]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Kaposi sarcoma</strong>: monitor <strong>CD4 count and HIV viral load</strong> in AIDS-KS, and watch for <strong>KS-IRIS</strong> after starting ART<sup class=\"cite\">[17,21]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>", "subs": []}, {"id": "8", "title": "Vaccines and Prophylaxis", "html": "", "subs": [{"id": "8.1", "title": "Vaccines", "html": "<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Vaccine</th>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Who receives it</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Varicella vaccine</strong> (monovalent, or <strong>MMRV</strong>)</td>\n<td><strong>Live attenuated</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Two doses</strong> in childhood (in Italy part of the mandatory schedule, MMRV at 13–15 months and 5–6 years<sup class=\"cite\">[7]</sup>); non-immune adolescents and adults, especially <strong>healthcare workers</strong> and <strong>women planning pregnancy</strong>. <strong>Contraindicated</strong> in <strong>pregnancy</strong> and <strong>severe immunosuppression</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[7,19]</sup></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Recombinant zoster vaccine (RZV)</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Non-live</strong>, recombinant <strong>glycoprotein E</strong> with the <strong>AS01B adjuvant</strong>; <strong>2 doses</strong> given 2 to 6 months apart</td>\n<td>USA (ACIP): adults <strong>≥50 years</strong> and <strong>immunocompromised adults ≥19 years</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[6]</sup>. Italy (PNPV 2023–2025): adults aged <strong>65</strong>, and <strong>at-risk people from 18 years</strong> (for example immunosuppression, diabetes, chronic lung, heart or kidney disease)<sup class=\"cite\">[7]</sup>. Can be given to people who have already had zoster</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Live zoster vaccine</strong></td>\n<td>Live attenuated</td>\n<td>Largely replaced by RZV; <strong>contraindicated in immunocompromised</strong> people</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>HSV, CMV, EBV, HHV-8</strong></td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td><strong>No licensed vaccine</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>"}, {"id": "8.2", "title": "Post-exposure prophylaxis for varicella", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Varicella vaccine</strong> within <strong>3 days (up to 5 days)</strong> of exposure for <strong>non-immune immunocompetent</strong> contacts without contraindications<sup class=\"cite\">[19]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Varicella-zoster immune globulin (VZIG)</strong> as soon as possible and <strong>within 10 days</strong> for <strong>high-risk non-immune</strong> contacts: <strong>immunocompromised</strong> people, <strong>pregnant women</strong>, <strong>newborns whose mother's varicella started 5 days before to 2 days after delivery</strong>, and some preterm infants<sup class=\"cite\">[20]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Infection control</strong>: <strong>airborne + contact precautions</strong> for varicella and <strong>disseminated zoster</strong> (and for zoster in immunocompromised patients); <strong>localized zoster</strong> in an immunocompetent patient: standard precautions with lesions <strong>covered</strong>. Non-immune staff should not care for these patients.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "8.3", "title": "Antiviral prophylaxis", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>HSV/VZV</strong>: <strong>acyclovir or valacyclovir prophylaxis</strong> in <strong>HSCT</strong> recipients, during intensive chemotherapy for leukemia, and with some drugs that raise zoster risk (for example <strong>proteasome inhibitors</strong>) in seropositive patients<sup class=\"cite\">[9]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>Genital herpes</strong>: <strong>daily suppressive therapy</strong> reduces transmission to an uninfected partner, together with <strong>condoms</strong> and <strong>avoiding sex during outbreaks</strong><sup class=\"cite\">[2,23]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>CMV in SOT</strong>: two strategies, <strong>universal prophylaxis</strong> (valganciclovir for a set period, typically 3 to 6 months, longer in high-risk settings; <strong>letermovir</strong> is an alternative in <strong>D+/R− kidney</strong> recipients) or <strong>pre-emptive therapy</strong> (regular QNAT surveillance and treatment when a threshold is crossed)<sup class=\"cite\">[8]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>CMV in allogeneic HSCT</strong>: <strong>letermovir prophylaxis</strong> for <strong>CMV-seropositive recipients</strong> reduced clinically significant CMV infection in a phase 3 trial, and is standard; pre-emptive monitoring continues<sup class=\"cite\">[9,10]</sup>. <strong>CMV-safe blood products</strong> (seronegative or <strong>leukoreduced</strong>) for at-risk patients<sup class=\"cite\">[9]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>CMV in HIV</strong>: no primary prophylaxis; the best prevention is <strong>ART</strong> with immune recovery<sup class=\"cite\">[21]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "8.4", "title": "Preventing congenital CMV", "html": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Hygiene counseling</strong> for pregnant women: avoid contact with <strong>saliva and urine of young children</strong> (do not share cutlery or kiss on the mouth; wash hands after nappy changes)<sup class=\"cite\">[12]</sup>.</li>\n<li><strong>High-dose oral valacyclovir</strong> for <strong>primary maternal infection around conception or in the first trimester</strong> reduces <strong>vertical transmission</strong>: in a randomized trial, fetal infection fell from 30% to 11%<sup class=\"cite\">[12,13]</sup>. The ECCI consensus recommends it<sup class=\"cite\">[12]</sup>.</li>\n</ul>"}]}], "open": ["Classify the human herpesviruses into the three subfamilies and state the site of latency and one key disease for each virus.", "Explain the cycle of primary infection, latency and reactivation, using VZV (varicella and zoster) as an example.", "Describe the clinical presentation, diagnostic work-up and immediate management of suspected HSV encephalitis.", "How is genital herpes diagnosed, and what are the roles and limits of type-specific serology?", "Compare episodic and suppressive therapy for recurrent genital herpes, and describe how to manage genital herpes in pregnancy.", "Describe the clinical features and complications of herpes zoster, including herpes zoster ophthalmicus, Ramsay Hunt syndrome and postherpetic neuralgia.", "Describe the clinical picture, laboratory diagnosis (including the interpretation of EBV serology) and management of infectious mononucleosis.", "Which patients are at risk of CMV disease, and what are the main clinical manifestations of CMV in immunocompromised patients?", "Describe the diagnosis, treatment and prevention of congenital CMV infection.", "List the diseases caused by HHV-6 and HHV-8 and describe the epidemiological forms of Kaposi sarcoma."], "refs": "<ol>\n<li>Bennett JE, Dolin R, Blaser MJ, eds. <em>Mandell, Douglas, and Bennett's Principles and Practice of Infectious Diseases</em>. 9th ed. Philadelphia: Elsevier; 2020. Chapters on herpes simplex virus, varicella-zoster virus, cytomegalovirus, Epstein-Barr virus, human herpesviruses 6, 7 and 8.</li>\n<li>Workowski KA, Bachmann LH, Chan PA, et al. Sexually Transmitted Infections Treatment Guidelines, 2021. <em>MMWR Recomm Rep</em>. 2021;70(4):1-187. https://doi.org/10.15585/mmwr.rr7004a1</li>\n<li>Gnann JW Jr, Whitley RJ. Genital herpes. <em>N Engl J Med</em>. 2016;375(7):666-674. https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMcp1603178</li>\n<li>Granerod J, Sellner J, et al. Diagnosis and management of infectious encephalitis in adults: guidelines from the European Academy of Neurology and European Society of Clinical Microbiology and Infectious Diseases. <em>Eur J Neurol</em>. 2026;33(10):e70647. https://doi.org/10.1111/ene.70647</li>\n<li>Cohen JI. Herpes zoster. <em>N Engl J Med</em>. 2013;369(3):255-263. https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMcp1302674</li>\n<li>Anderson TC, Masters NB, Guo A, et al. Use of recombinant zoster vaccine in immunocompromised adults aged ≥19 years: recommendations of the Advisory Committee on Immunization Practices — United States, 2022. <em>MMWR Morb Mortal Wkly Rep</em>. 2022;71(3):80-84. https://doi.org/10.15585/mmwr.mm7103a2</li>\n<li>Ministero della Salute. Piano Nazionale di Prevenzione Vaccinale (PNPV) 2023–2025 and Calendario vaccinale. <em>Gazzetta Ufficiale</em> Serie Generale n. 194, 21 August 2023. https://www.salute.gov.it</li>\n<li>Kotton CN, Kumar D, Manuel O, et al. The Fourth International Consensus Guidelines on the Management of Cytomegalovirus in Solid Organ Transplantation. <em>Transplantation</em>. 2025;109(7):1066-1110. https://doi.org/10.1097/TP.0000000000005374</li>\n<li>Ljungman P, de la Camara R, Robin C, et al. Guidelines for the management of cytomegalovirus infection in patients with haematological malignancies and after stem cell transplantation from the 2017 European Conference on Infections in Leukaemia (ECIL 7). <em>Lancet Infect Dis</em>. 2019;19(8):e260-e272. https://doi.org/10.1016/S1473-3099(19)30107-0</li>\n<li>Marty FM, Ljungman P, Chemaly RF, et al. Letermovir prophylaxis for cytomegalovirus in hematopoietic-cell transplantation. <em>N Engl J Med</em>. 2017;377(25):2433-2444. https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa1706640</li>\n<li>Avery RK, Alain S, Alexander BD, et al. Maribavir for refractory cytomegalovirus infections with or without resistance post-transplant: results from a phase 3 randomized clinical trial. <em>Clin Infect Dis</em>. 2022;75(4):690-701. https://doi.org/10.1093/cid/ciab988</li>\n<li>Leruez-Ville M, Chatzakis C, Lilleri D, et al. Consensus recommendation for prenatal, neonatal and postnatal management of congenital cytomegalovirus infection from the European congenital infection initiative (ECCI). <em>Lancet Reg Health Eur</em>. 2024;40:100892. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.lanepe.2024.100892</li>\n<li>Shahar-Nissan K, Pardo J, Peled O, et al. Valaciclovir to prevent vertical transmission of cytomegalovirus after maternal primary infection during pregnancy: a randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial. <em>Lancet</em>. 2020;396(10253):779-785. https://doi.org/10.1016/S0140-6736(20)31868-7</li>\n<li>Kimberlin DW, Jester PM, Sánchez PJ, et al. Valganciclovir for symptomatic congenital cytomegalovirus disease. <em>N Engl J Med</em>. 2015;372(10):933-943. https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa1404599</li>\n<li>Dunmire SK, Verghese PS, Balfour HH Jr. Primary Epstein-Barr virus infection. <em>J Clin Virol</em>. 2018;102:84-92. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jcv.2018.03.001</li>\n<li>Agut H, Bonnafous P, Gautheret-Dejean A. Laboratory and clinical aspects of human herpesvirus 6 infections. <em>Clin Microbiol Rev</em>. 2015;28(2):313-335. https://doi.org/10.1128/CMR.00122-14</li>\n<li>Cesarman E, Damania B, Krown SE, et al. Kaposi sarcoma. <em>Nat Rev Dis Primers</em>. 2019;5(1):9. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41572-019-0060-9</li>\n<li>World Health Organization. Herpes simplex virus. Fact sheet. https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/herpes-simplex-virus</li>\n<li>Marin M, Güris D, Chaves SS, et al. Prevention of varicella: recommendations of the Advisory Committee on Immunization Practices (ACIP). <em>MMWR Recomm Rep</em>. 2007;56(RR-4):1-40. https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/rr5604a1.htm</li>\n<li>Centers for Disease Control and Prevention. Updated recommendations for use of VariZIG — United States, 2013. <em>MMWR Morb Mortal Wkly Rep</em>. 2013;62(28):574-576. https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/mm6228a4.htm</li>\n<li>Panel on Guidelines for the Prevention and Treatment of Opportunistic Infections in Adults and Adolescents with HIV. Guidelines for the Prevention and Treatment of Opportunistic Infections in Adults and Adolescents with HIV (CMV and Kaposi sarcoma sections). NIH, CDC, HIVMA/IDSA; updated continuously. https://clinicalinfo.hiv.gov/en/guidelines/hiv-clinical-guidelines-adult-and-adolescent-opportunistic-infections</li>\n<li>Bundesinstitut für Arzneimittel und Medizinprodukte (BfArM). Rote-Hand-Brief: Brivudin — potentially fatal interaction with fluoropyrimidines. 2020. https://www.bfarm.de/SharedDocs/Risikoinformationen/Pharmakovigilanz/EN/RHB/2020/rhb-brivudin.pdf</li>\n<li>Corey L, Wald A, Patel R, et al. Once-daily valacyclovir to reduce the risk of transmission of genital herpes. <em>N Engl J Med</em>. 2004;350(1):11-20. https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa035144</li>\n</ol>"};
